.TH C-ARBINT 3 "@MAN_DATE@" "c-arbint @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
c-arbint \- arbitrary-precision integer library for C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <arbint.h>
.fi
.PP
Link with:
.PP
.nf
.B cc ... $(pkg-config --cflags --libs c-arbint)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c-arbint
provides signed arbitrary-precision integer arithmetic with explicit
allocator-aware object management.
.PP
This page documents the functions currently exported by
.BR libarbint .
Additional declarations may exist in
.BR arbint.h
for future releases.
.SH TYPES
.TP
.B arbint_t
Opaque integer object type (single-object array form). Initialize with
.BR arbint_init ()
and release with
.BR arbint_clear ().
.TP
.B arbint_ctx_t
Execution/allocation context. A context carries the allocator used for object
storage and for exported buffers.
.TP
.B arbint_err_t
Error code enum used by mutating/extracting APIs.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.PP
Functions returning
.B arbint_err_t
use
.B ARBINT_OK
on success and a nonzero error code on failure.
.PP
Predicate/comparison helpers return
.B int
(typically boolean-style 0/1 or three-way compare \-1/0/+1).
.SH ERROR CODES
.TP
.B ARBINT_EINVAL
Invalid argument (for example NULL where not accepted, or invalid object state).
.TP
.B ARBINT_ENOMEM
Allocation failure.
.TP
.B ARBINT_EOVERFLOW
Value/capacity overflow for the requested conversion or operation.
.TP
.B ARBINT_ESIGN
Unsigned extraction requested from a negative value.
.TP
.B ARBINT_EZERO
Division by zero.
.SH IMPLEMENTED API
.SS Context and Object Lifetime
.BR arbint_ctx_init_default (),
.BR arbint_ctx_init (),
.BR arbint_ctx_clear (),
.BR arbint_init (),
.BR arbint_clear (),
.BR arbint_get_ctx (),
.BR arbint_set_ctx (),
.BR arbint_zero (),
.BR arbint_resize (),
.BR arbint_swap ()
.PP
These APIs initialize/destroy contexts and integer objects, rebind objects to a
new context, zero values, reserve limb capacity, and swap complete object state.
.SS Assignment and Extraction
.BR arbint_set (),
.BR arbint_set_i32 (),
.BR arbint_set_u32 (),
.BR arbint_get_i32 (),
.BR arbint_get_u32 ()
.PP
Assign from another integer or a 32-bit scalar; extract into 32-bit scalar
forms with range/sign checking.
.SS Fit Checks
.BR arbint_fits_u8 (),
.BR arbint_fits_u16 (),
.BR arbint_fits_u32 (),
.BR arbint_fits_u64 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i8 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i16 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i32 (),
.BR arbint_fits_i64 ()
.PP
Non-allocating representability predicates.
.SS Sign and Basic Predicates
.BR arbint_signum (),
.BR arbint_is_zero (),
.BR arbint_is_one (),
.BR arbint_is_neg (),
.BR arbint_is_odd (),
.BR arbint_is_even (),
.BR arbint_abs (),
.BR arbint_neg ()
.PP
Sign/shape queries and sign transforms.
.SS Comparison
.BR arbint_cmp (),
.BR arbint_cmpabs (),
.BR arbint_cmp_i32 (),
.BR arbint_cmp_u32 (),
.BR arbint_eq (),
.BR arbint_ne (),
.BR arbint_lt (),
.BR arbint_le (),
.BR arbint_gt (),
.BR arbint_ge (),
.BR arbint_eq_i32 (),
.BR arbint_ne_i32 (),
.BR arbint_lt_i32 (),
.BR arbint_le_i32 (),
.BR arbint_gt_i32 (),
.BR arbint_ge_i32 (),
.BR arbint_eq_u32 (),
.BR arbint_ne_u32 (),
.BR arbint_lt_u32 (),
.BR arbint_le_u32 (),
.BR arbint_gt_u32 (),
.BR arbint_ge_u32 ()
.PP
Three-way and predicate comparisons against big integers and 32-bit scalars.
.SS Arithmetic
.BR arbint_add (),
.BR arbint_sub (),
.BR arbint_mul (),
.BR arbint_sqr (),
.BR arbint_add_i32 (),
.BR arbint_add_u32 (),
.BR arbint_sub_i32 (),
.BR arbint_sub_u32 ()
.PP
Core signed arithmetic on arbitrary-size integers.
.SS Truncated Division and Remainder
.BR arbint_tdiv_qr (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_q (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_r (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_qr_u32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_q_u32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_r_u32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_qr_i32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_q_i32 (),
.BR arbint_tdiv_r_i32 ()
.PP
Truncated division obeys:
.PP
.nf
q = trunc(n / d)
r = n - q*d
.fi
.PP
with
.I |r| < |d|
and remainder sign matching the dividend (or zero).
.SS Binary Import and Export
.BR arbint_import (),
.BR arbint_export ()
.PP
Convert between
.B arbint_t
and a little-endian magnitude byte buffer.
.B arbint_export ()
exports magnitude only (no sign encoding).
.SH ALIASING RULES
.PP
For single-output operations, destination may alias an input.
.PP
For two-output division APIs, output aliases are accepted; if both outputs are
the same object, the last-written output value remains in that object.
.SH MEMORY NOTES
.PP
.B arbint_export ()
allocates the output buffer using the allocator in the source object's context.
The caller owns that buffer and must free it with the same allocator contract.
.SH ALGORITHMIC NOTES
.PP
Multiplication dispatches between a generic implementation and an optimized
BMI2 path on supported x86 targets, switching between the naive
schoolbook - O(n^2) - and Karatsuba - O(n^log2(3))-  algorithms based
on operand size.
.PP
Division uses magnitude long division and then applies sign rules for
truncation semantics.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <arbint.h>

arbint_ctx_t ctx;
arbint_t a, b, q, r;

arbint_ctx_init_default(&ctx);
arbint_init(a, &ctx);
arbint_init(b, &ctx);
arbint_init(q, &ctx);
arbint_init(r, &ctx);

arbint_set_i32(a, -17);
arbint_set_i32(b, 5);
arbint_tdiv_qr(q, r, a, b); /* q = -3, r = -2 */

arbint_clear(a);
arbint_clear(b);
arbint_clear(q);
arbint_clear(r);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkg-config (1)
